    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A prince-electorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, ruled from 1423 by the Wettin dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Wise</a> protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> the cradle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a>.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus the Strong</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1697, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first hard-paste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Erzgebirge</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> was struck from about 1500 into some of the first large silver coins of the type later called the thaler, the name that lives on in the word dollar.</p></aside>
    </section>

