    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-elector-of-saxony"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-iii-elector-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Albert III, Elector of Saxony" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-elector-of-saxony">Albert III, Elector of Saxony</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_der_Beherzte,_1443-1500_(AT_KHM_GG4796).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> began as a small territory with a great title. After the old Duchy of Saxony was broken up in 1180, the name and ducal rank passed to the Ascanian dukes, whose line divided into Saxe-Lauenburg and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> in 1356 settled their dispute over the right to elect the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in favour of Duke Rudolf I of Saxe-Wittenberg, making him one of the seven prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arch-marshal-of-the-holy-roman-empire">Arch-Marshal of the Empire</a>, with the lands around Wittenberg on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> attached to the dignity. When the Ascanian electoral line died out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-elector-of-saxony">Albert III</a> in 1422, Emperor Sigismund granted the electorate in 1423 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Warlike</a>, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a>, as a reward for his support against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>. This joined the electoral title to the much larger and richer Wettin lands of Meissen and, from 1440, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, and the name Saxony gradually spread to all of them. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/division-of-leipzig">Division of Leipzig</a> of 1485 the brothers Ernest and Albert split the inheritance: Ernest kept the electorate with Wittenberg and most of Thuringia, while Albert received Meissen with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine line</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/freiberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/freiberg.jpg" alt="Freiberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freiberg-Petri-Obermarkt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kolossos, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was one of the most prosperous and urbanised territories of the Empire. Its wealth began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Erzgebirge</a>, mined at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a> since the twelfth century and, after new strikes from the 1470s, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schneeberg-saxony">Schneeberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaberg-buchholz">Annaberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marienberg-fortress">Marienberg</a>; the large silver coins struck there from about 1500 were among the first of the type later called the thaler. Mining brought technical expertise, recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgius-agricola">Georg Agricola</a>’s <em>De re metallica</em> of 1556, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg-mining-academy">Freiberg Mining Academy</a>, founded in 1765, became a leading school of mining science. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> grew into a major trade fair city, linking eastern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and from the seventeenth century it was the centre of the German book trade. Textiles, linen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, glass and, after 1710, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> manufactory developed into important industries, making Saxony a forerunner of German industrialisation. The universities of Leipzig, founded in 1409, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, founded in 1502, made the region a centre of learning. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> Saxony was strictly Lutheran, and the church was organised under consistories controlled by the elector. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> worked as cantor of St Thomas’s Church in Leipzig from 1723 until his death in 1750, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> became a city of court music, painting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architecture.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg.jpg" alt="Battle of Mühlberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_M%C3%BChlberg_1547.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LUIS DE AVILA Y ZUNIGA (c.1490 C. 1560), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a medium-sized power in the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was often drawn into wars it could not control. The Ernestine elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-frederick-i-elector-of-saxony">John Frederick I</a> led the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>, and his defeat and capture by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a> in 1547 cost his line the electoral title. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-elector-of-saxony">Maurice of Saxony</a>, who had sided with the emperor and received the electorate, turned against him in the Princes’ Revolt of 1552, forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a>, and died in 1553 from wounds received at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sievershausen">Battle of Sievershausen</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-elector-of-saxony">John George I</a> first stayed neutral, then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> contingent fled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-breitenfeld-1631">Battle of Breitenfeld</a> in 1631, and Saxony suffered heavily from both sides before its separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> with the emperor in 1635. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-iii-elector-of-saxony">John George III</a> led Saxon troops to the relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus the Strong</a>’s involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> brought a Swedish invasion and the humiliating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-altranstadt-1706">Treaty of Altranstädt</a> in 1706. In the Silesian Wars Saxony was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kesselsdorf">Kesselsdorf</a> in 1745, and in 1756 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> invaded without warning; the Saxon army capitulated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirna">Pirna</a> and was forced into Prussian service, and the country remained occupied for most of the Seven Years’ War.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong.jpg" alt="Augustus II the Strong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II the Strong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_de_Silvestre-August_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis de Silvestre, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The elector governed as a territorial prince within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, holding a vote in imperial elections and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, leading the Protestant estates at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>, a role it formally kept as director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-evangelicorum">Corpus Evangelicorum</a> even after the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus the Strong</a>. Government developed early into a structured administration: a privy council, a central treasury and, from the sixteenth century, a consistory to govern the church. Elector Augustus (r. 1553–1586) was a notable administrator who reorganised finances and courts, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Constitutions of 1572, a codification of law, and founded the Kunstkammer that grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>’s state collections. The estates of nobles, prelates and towns met in diets (<em>Landtage</em>) and kept control over taxation, which limited the elector’s absolutism. Junior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine lines</a> were endowed in 1656–1657 with the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-merseburg">Saxe-Merseburg</a> and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>, which reverted to the electorate when they died out in the eighteenth century. In 1697 Frederick Augustus I, known as Augustus the Strong, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> to be elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, beginning a personal union that his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> continued until 1763; the electorate remained Lutheran, guaranteed by the estates. After the Seven Years’ War the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-retablissement">Rétablissement</a></em>, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-christian-elector-of-saxony">Frederick Christian</a> and continued under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-i-of-saxony">Frederick Augustus III</a>, reduced debt, reformed finances and encouraged industry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zwinger-dresden"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zwinger-dresden.jpg" alt="Zwinger (Dresden)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zwinger-dresden">Zwinger (Dresden)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2006-07-30_Zwinger_dresden2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stephan Czuratis (Jazz-face), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The electorate’s greatest impact on world history was the protection it gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Wise</a>, who had founded the university where Luther taught, refused to hand him over after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in 1521 and hid him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a>, where Luther translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Lutheran Reformation</a> was first institutionalised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> visitations from 1527, which became a model for Protestant churches, and the language of the Saxon chancery, used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luther-bible">Luther’s Bible</a>, contributed greatly to the formation of standard written German. Saxon theologians shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/formula-of-concord">Formula of Concord</a> of 1577 and Lutheran orthodoxy. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus the Strong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> became one of the finest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwinger-dresden">Zwinger</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden-frauenkirche">Frauenkirche</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-vault">Green Vault</a> treasury and a picture gallery that acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-madonna">Sistine Madonna</a> in 1754. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-friedrich-bottger">Johann Friedrich Böttger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehrenfried-walther-von-tschirnhaus">Ehrenfried Walther von Tschirnhaus</a> produced the first European hard-paste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, and the manufactory founded in 1710 set a model for royal porcelain works across the continent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>’s industrial and commercial strength later made it one of the most industrialised regions of nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and today the Free State of Saxony preserves its name, its capital and its collections.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-bruhl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-bruhl.jpg" alt="Heinrich von Brühl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-bruhl">Heinrich von Brühl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcello_Bacciarelli_-_Heinrich_Graf_von_Br%C3%BChl.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcello Bacciarelli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>’s relative decline began in the eighteenth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> overtook it as the leading Protestant power of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> proved costly: the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus the Strong</a>, the expense of royal display and the government of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-bruhl">Heinrich von Brühl</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> left large debts, and the Seven Years’ War brought occupation, heavy requisitions and the destruction of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> in the Prussian bombardment of 1760. With the death of Augustus III in 1763 the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> ended. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-retablissement">Rétablissement</a></em> restored the finances, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-i-of-saxony">Frederick Augustus III</a> kept Saxony at peace for most of his reign, joining Prussia in the War of the Bavarian Succession of 1778–1779 and declining the Polish crown offered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>. When war between Prussia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> broke out in 1806, Saxony sided with Prussia, and its army shared in the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> in October 1806. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-posen">Treaty of Posen</a> in December 1806 Frederick Augustus made peace with Napoleon, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and took the title of king, ending the electorate a few months after the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. The Kingdom of Saxony that replaced it lost the northern part of its territory, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, to Prussia in 1815.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was the territory that protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and first organised a Lutheran state church; rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining and trade, it made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> a centre of commerce and music and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> capital, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> overshadowed it in the eighteenth century.</em></p>

